    body: '- Simile — a comparison using like or as\n- Metaphor — one thing stated to be another, without like or as\n- Personification — a human quality given to a non-human thing\n- Onomatopoeia — words that imitate the sounds they describe\n- Alliteration — repetition of the same initial consonant sound in neighbouring words\n- Assonance — repetition of vowel sounds in nearby words\n- Consonance — repetition of similar consonant sounds at the ends of nearby words\n- Hyperbole — deliberate exaggeration for effect\n- Understatement — presenting something as less important than it is; litotes does it by a double negative\n- Oxymoron — two contradictory terms placed together, as in deafening silence\n- Paradox — a self-contradictory statement that reveals a deeper truth\n- Pun — a humorous play on the different meanings of one word\n- Apostrophe — addressing an absent person or abstract quality as if present\n- Metonymy — substituting a linked term, such as the crown for the monarchy\n- Synecdoche — a part standing for the whole, such as sails for a ship\n- Transferred epithet — the description moves from the person onto an object, as in a sleepless night\n- Zeugma — one verb governing two objects, as in she broke his car and his heart\n- Antithesis — contrasting ideas set in parallel structure\n- Anaphora — repetition at the start of successive lines; epistrophe repeats at the end\n- Chiasmus — reversal of word order in two paired clauses\n- Polysyndeton — deliberate use of many conjunctions; asyndeton omits them\n- Conceit — an elaborate, extended metaphor carried through a passage\n- Rhetorical question — a question asked to make a point, not to get an answer\n- Pathetic fallacy — human emotions attributed to nature\n- Satire — humour or ridicule used to expose vice; parody imitates a style for laughter\n- Imagery by sense: visual, auditory, olfactory, tactile, gustatory'
