    title: 'Poetic forms and line structures',
    body: '- Sonnet — 14 lines of iambic pentameter; Petrarchan has octave plus sestet, Shakespearean three quatrains and a couplet\n- Shakespearean rhyme scheme — ABAB CDCD EFEF GG\n- Petrarchan octave — ABBAABBA, called enclosed rhyme\n- Villanelle — 5 tercets and a final quatrain with two recurring refrains\n- Sestina — 6 six-line stanzas with six end-words recurring in set order\n- Ballad — a story in song-like stanzas; elegy mourns the dead; ode praises a noble subject\n- Epic — long narrative of heroic deeds; pastoral — idealised country life; lyric — short and musical, expressing personal feeling\n- Dramatic monologue — a speaker other than the poet reveals himself to a silent listener\n- Blank verse — unrhymed iambic pentameter; free verse — no fixed meter or rhyme\n- Heroic couplet — two rhymed iambic pentameter lines; limerick — five lines, usually AABBA\n- Foot — the basic unit of meter; iamb unstressed then stressed, trochee stressed then unstressed\n- Dactyl — one stressed plus two unstressed; anapest — two unstressed plus one stressed; spondee — two stresses\n- Pentameter — 5 feet to a line; tetrameter — 4 feet\n- Couplet 2 lines, tercet 3, quatrain 4, sestet 6, octave 8\n- Caesura — a pause inside a line; enjambment — the thought runs over the line end; end-stopped — the line is grammatically complete\n- Stanza — a group of lines set off by blank space; refrain — a line repeated at intervals\n- Rhyme: end, internal, eye, slant, masculine (ends stressed), feminine (ends unstressed), monorhyme (all lines alike)\n- Greek ode sections — strophe, antistrophe, epode'
